TI  - Highly oxidation-resistant and rapid-gelation conductive hydrogels based on Fe3O4-assisted dopamine acrylamide@MXene for wearable sensors
T2  - CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA  - English
KW  - Highly oxidation-resistant
KW  - MXene
KW  - Rapid gelation
KW  - Hydrogel
KW  - Density functional theory
KW  - Wearable sensors
AB  - MXene is widely used in the preparation of conductive hydrogels due to its excellent electrical conductivity and hydrophilicity. However, in the precursor solution and hydrogel preparation processes, it is unavoidably oxidized, particularly when rapid gelation is facilitated by high concentrations of ammonium persulfate (APS), resulting in reduced conductivity. Herein, high oxidation-resistant and rapid-gelation hydrogels are fabricated without external energy input via Fe3O4 -assisted dopamine acrylamide (DAM)@MXene. DAM, both a monomer and a reducing agent, possesses high affinity for MXene as verified via density functional theory, significantly delaying MXene oxidation through chemical interactions and physical isolation. Furthermore, it is also found that an order of magnitude lower APS concentration is sufficient to achieve rapid gelation with the synergistic effect of Fe3O4, which effectively reduces MXene oxidation, lowers the cost and improves production efficiency. Subsequently, flexible, self-healing, adhesive, and biocompatible poly(acrylic acid-dopamine acrylamide-acrylic phenylboronic acid)/polyvinyl alcohol (PADA/PVA) hydrogels are successfully prepared and used as wearable sensors with high sensitivity (GF = 11.4), wide sensing (0 similar to 500 %) range, fast response (125 ms), and low detection limit (0.5 %). This universal high oxidation-resistant and rapid-gelation method gives novel insights for the efficient preparation of the next-generation wearable sensors.
