AB  - Flexible wearable sensors based on bio-materials have attracted widespread attention for use in electronic skin, human motion/heath monitoring, et al. However, the limitations of most current sensor materials greatly restrict their application as bio-flexible sensors. The development of bio-flexible sensors based on natural polymeric materials with structures similar to human skin is the key to solving this problem. In this study, a hydrogel-based wearable bio-flexible strain/pressure sensor was designed and successfully developed. Firstly, Silk fibroin (SF, a natural polymer material), served as the base, while polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) and tannic acid (TA) were used as crosslinkers, and polyethylene glycol (PEG) was used as a reinforcement to enhance the mechanical strength of the hydrogel. The developed wearable strain/pressure sensor (PSTP) exhibited good mechanical strength, high sensitivity and fast response time. When the sensors are attached to the surface of human skin, large deformations (e.g., deformations of finger joints and wrists) and small deformations (e.g., pulse movements) can be monitored. In addition, the bio-flexible strain/pressure sensor enabled the transmission of encrypted information at certain distances using Morse code. Therefore, this bio-flexible strain/pressure sensor has potential applications in strain sensing, pressure sensing, information transmission, and human motion monitoring, and also provides a feasible reference for the development of multifunctional bio wearable sensors based on natural polymer materials.
