AB  - It is pivotal and challenging to tailor-make compressible and water-locking hydrogels in pursuit of reliable and stable wearable sensors. Herein, the double network hydrogels based on tetrahedral borate-complexed polyvinyl alcohol (PB) and polymerizable deep eutectic solvent (PD) have been presented. The mutual entanglement of PB and PD networks minimizes the energy dissipation with synchronous increment of non-freezing bound water content, which endows hydrogels with superior compressive and high water-locking performance. The hydrogels could be compressed up to 80 % with the strength of 1.31 f 0.06 MPa even at water content of more than 80 wt %, which is significantly improved when compared with those of single network counterparts of PB hydrogel (80 %, 0.04 f 0.004 MPa) and PD hydrogel (stock-break failure mode at 68.74 f 3.35 %, 0.87 f 0.10 MPa). The double network hydrogels could withstand successive compression at 50 % strain over 100 cycles with maintenance of 98.55 % weight. High compressibility and superior water-locking performance combined with the excellent conductivity, adhesiveness and antibacterial properties enables the hydrogels to monitor human motions. This work provides one promising approach to the construction of compressible and water-locking hydrogels based on the entanglement of double networks for the development of advanced wearable sensors.
