KW  - Soret effect
KW  - NANOGENERATORS
KW  - REMOVAL
AB  - Ionic thermoelectric hydrogels (i-TEHs) hold great promise for low-grade thermal energy harvesting and self-powered sensing, but their applications are limited by the trade-off between ionic conductivity and the Seebeck coefficient. A method combining electrohydrodynamic (EHD) printing technology and cyclic freeze-thaw processes was proposed to fabricate a polyvinyl alcohol/sodium carboxymethyl cellulose/sodium bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl) imide (PVA/CMC-Na/NaTFSI) i-TEH for self-powered wearable sensors. I-TEH was constructed with an interpenetrating network of PVA and CMC-Na, using NaTFSI as the mobile ions to promote thermal diffusion. Ions in i-TEHs were uniformly distributed by EHD printing process, resulting in an ionic conductivity of 34.27 mS cm(-1), which is a 53.5 % increase compared to the traditional methods. The synergistic effect between ordered ions and Manning counterion condensation effect induced by the electronegative CMC-Na chain improves the selective ion migration capability of i-TEHs, achieving a high Seebeck coefficient of 14.39 mV K-1 (reference temperature of 25-40 degrees C, temperature difference of 10-15 K), which is a 24.6 % increase compared to the traditional method. The i-TEH-based self-powered sensor exhibits a gauge factor of 2.09 within the strain range of 150-200 % and fast response/recovery time (102/129 ms). The i-THE-based self-powered flexible sensor can effectively monitor human movements and handwriting recognition. The synergistic effect of ordered ion arrangement induced by EHD printing and Manning counterion condensation effect provides a feasible strategy for improving the TE performance of i-TEHs, which opens up a new approach for the preparation of self-powered flexible wearable electronic devices based on human body thermal energy.
