AB  - Due to their outstanding flexibility and biocompatibility, conductive hydrogels hold significant promise for intelligent wearable sensor applications in health monitoring systems. However, conventional hydrogels suffer from the poor mechanical strength, low sensitivity, and interfacial stress limitations. Here, a multifunctional Polyvinyl alcohol-Borax/Graphene Oxide-Cellulose Nanofiber(PBGC) conductive hydrogel was developed through strategic integration of borate ester bonds and hydrogen bonding. The network established a dual conductive mechanism with electron-dominated and ion-assisted characteristics. To verify the PBGC hydrogel properties, mechanical performance testing, electrical conductivity testing, and current-time (I-T) current signal testing were conducted. The addition of Borax enabled PBGC hydrogel to exhibit excellent maximum tensile strength (0.96 MPa), fracture elongation (512.8 %), and toughness (48.13 MJ m-3). The incorporation of GO and CNF significantly improved the conductivity (1.03 S m-1) and strain sensitivity (749 %). Moreover, chitosan integration endows the material with robust interfacial adhesion and sustained biocompatibility. This novel conductive hydrogel established a reliable foundation for the application of wearable electronics in medical monitoring and e-skin through its innovative material architecture.
