TI  - A hydrogel-based SERS sensor with wearable potential and machine learning integration for sweat stimulant detection
T2  - CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA  - English
KW  - Wearable SERS sensor
KW  - Hydrogel
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Au@Ag NRs
KW  - Stimulants
KW  - SALBUTAMOL
AB  - Doping abuse presents a persistent challenge in competitive sports and public health. To address the limitations of conventional detection methods, we developed a flexible, skin-adhesive hydrogel-based SERS sensor with wearable potential for non-invasive, on-body detection of doping agents in sweat. The ASP hydrogel (Au@Ag NRs/S-CNF/PAA/PAAM) was fabricated by embedding sulfonated cellulose nanofiber (S-CNF)-stabilized Au@Ag NRs into a PAA/PAAM double-network matrix. This design enhances nanoparticle dispersion, mechanical flexibility, and sweat absorption. The sensor achieved detection limits of 1.75 x 10(-8) M (HCTZ), 1.0 x 10(-9) M (TMZ), and 8.86 x 10(-8) M (SAL), with excellent signal uniformity (RSD = 4.58 %) and 60-day storage stability. Mechanical tests confirmed optimized tensile strain (176.6 %) and good adhesive strength. The ASP hydrogel also demonstrated strong antibacterial activity against both E. coli and S. aureus (inhibition rate > 99 %) and high biocompatibility (cell viability >96 %), ensuring long-term skin safety during wear. Furthermore, machine learning algorithms enabled accurate spectral classification of the three stimulants, achieving 100 % test accuracy and AUC = 1.0. This multifunctional hydrogel platform integrates high sensitivity, mechanical durability, antimicrobial protection, and intelligent data processing, offering strong potential for real-time on-body stimulant monitoring in anti-doping, sports medicine, and personalized health management.
