TI  - Hofmeister effect and dual-dynamic-bond cross-linking interaction enhanced ionic conductive and balanced mechanical properties hydrogels for flexible sensors
T2  - COLLOIDS AND SURFACES A-PHYSICOCHEMICAL AND ENGINEERING ASPECTS
LA  - English
KW  - Hydrogel
KW  - Hofmeister effect
KW  - Flexible wearable sensors
AB  - High conductivity and large mechanical strength are important parameters for soft electronic applications. However, it is a challenge to find a material with balanced electronic and mechanical performance. Herein, a novel conductive hydrogel (PCB) consisting of polyvinyl alcohol (PVA), carbonylated nanofibrillated cellulose (CNF), and borax based on Hofmeister effect and dual-dynamic-bond cross-linking interaction is developed, which simultaneously realize mechanical strength, balanced conductivity, and versatile properties. The comprehensive performances are broadly and simultaneously altered via tuning the aggregation states of polymer chains by kosmotropes or chaotropes. With various ions, the conductivity and mechanical performance are continuously in situ modulated over a large window: conductivity from 0.58 to 13.83 S m-1, strength from 0.1 to 17.30 MPa and modulus from 0.87 to 34.02 kPa. The ion transport process is inseparable from the changes of water content and pore structures caused by cross-linking density. Meanwhile, the mechanical performances greatly depend on the densification or loosing of polymer chains and crystalline domains. Furthermore, the resulted hydrogels are applied as wearable electronic sensors in health monitor, manipulator operation and handwriting recognition applications. This study presents a new strategy for designing multifunctional hydrogels and establishes a transformative platform for next-generation flexible bioelectronics and wearable sensing technologies.
