AB  - Grape fruit waste, containing abundant tannic acid (TA), has been the focus of much research and commercial interests in value-added products manufacture. This paper successfully prepared laser-induced graphene (LIG) by directly etching a composite substrate of TA and polyvinyl alcohol using a CO2 laser. In this study, the laser direct writing technique successfully converted TA into LIG at room temperature and pressure. By optimizing the laser power, adjusting the amount of TA, and modifying the laser focal length, graphene with low resistivity (37 +/- 3 Omega /sq) was achieved. The formed LIG had a porous foam-like structure with a low resistivity and high sensitivity, influenced by TA content, focal length, laser power, and rotated angle. These properties make this LIG a multifunctional material for applications in supercapacitor, triboelectric nanogenerator, Joule heater, and wearable sensor. In conclusion, this direct laser-induced conversion of renewable natural TA into graphene-based materials can potentially expand new business opportunities by recycling grape fruit waste, and accelerate the promotion of low-cost graphene.
