TI  - Breathable, porous and conductive e-patch for ECG sensing by rapid solution spraying method
T2  - POLYMER
LA  - English
KW  - Porous hydrogel
KW  - Solution spraying method
KW  - ECG sensor
AB  - Conductive hydrogels hold significant potential for applications in wearable sensors for detecting human motion and monitoring health diagnostics due to their flexible and self-adhesive properties. However, they often suffer from complex fabrication processes, high costs, and poor breathability. This paper proposes a rapid preparation method based on solution spraying to create ion-conductive hydrogels with an open porous structure for use as an e-patch in electrocardiogram (ECG) sensing. A glutaraldehyde (GA) solution containing NaCl ionic liquid is sprayed onto a mixed powder of polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) and tannic acid (TA) to prepare the porous hydrogel. The resulting porous hydrogel exhibits outstanding solution absorption and permeability, which is 13.5 times greater than that of traditional non-porous hydrogels, while maintaining a fracture strain of 153 %, a tensile strength of 368 kPa, and an adhesion strength of 5.28 kPa. By incorporating the NaCl ionic solution, the hydrogel achieves an electrical conductivity of 0.12 S/m and can rapidly respond to deformation within 81.2 ms. The porous e-patch can detect human motion in real time and acquire real-time ECG signals. Notably, the method of rapidly preparing porous hydrogels by spraying solutions onto powders enables an "instant spray and use" effect, significantly expanding its potential applications.
