AB  - Hydrogels have emerged as an ideal platform for wearable sensors due to their exceptional biocompatibility and flexibility, enabling seamless integration into multifunctional daily applications. Nevertheless, concurrently achieving hydrogels-based sensors with robust adhesion, efficient self-healing, high sensitivity, and broad strain detection range via a straightforward fabrication methodology remains a significant challenge. Here, we present a highly stretchable, adhesive and self-healing conductive Polyvinyl alcohol-Borax-Polypyrrole (PVA-B-PPy, PBP) hydrogel through in-situ polymerization and freeze-thaw treatment. The multi-synergistic dynamic interaction of reversible borate ester bonds, extensive hydrogen bonding and physically crosslinked PVA microcrystalline domains endows PBP hydrogel exceptional mechanical properties, tissue-mimetic softness, robust adhesion and autonomous self-healing (97.4 % mechanical efficiency, instantaneous electrical recovery). The PBP hydrogel-based strain sensor with a high sensitivity (GF = 2.48) and an extensive monitoring range (360 % strain) is capable to accurately and timely monitor various human movements. Furthermore, the PBP hydrogel enables long-term electrophysiological monitoring via skin-adhesive bioelectrodes and serves as human-machine interfaces through flexible pressure-sensor arrays and hydrogel touchscreen pens for smartphone operation. This research provides a promising strategy for designing hydrogels with tailored functionalities for wearable electronics that satisfy diverse requirements of practical applications in personalized healthcare monitoring, soft robotics, and interactive devices.
