TI  - A PAL in Need Is a PAL Indeed: An Opioid Use Case for a Peer Alerting Lifeline (PAL) to Mitigate Risk Exposure
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE FUTURE TECHNOLOGIES CONFERENCE (FTC) 2018, VOL 2
LA  - English
CP  - Future Technologies Conference (FTC)
KW  - Opioids
KW  - Naloxone
KW  - Public health
KW  - Social computing
KW  - Health applications
KW  - Mobile devices
AB  - Mobile phones hold the potential to mitigate risky behavior by helping establish peer-support networks prior to an emergency. Towards this goal, we have developed a social mobile application called the Peer Alerting Lifeline (PAL). Here we introduce a naloxone extension (NalPAL), designed to meet the needs of those at risk of an opioid overdose. The premise is that both health support and emergency responses could be tailored to an individual's unique situation by connecting them to a private and trusted network of contacts who are sympathetic to their specific needs. For instance, one unintended and adverse potential consequence of an ambulance response to an overdose is that it can jeopardize housing and shelter accommodation. A peer-response network could supplement mainstream emergency medical services by shortening response time, minimizing social stigma, and reducing threats to housing security. At this stage of the epidemic, the majority of opioid usage has shifted to rural and suburban areas where people are increasingly isolated and using opioids alone. Creative solutions must be found to support these individuals, many of whom are already beyond the reach of traditional health services. Community peer response via mobile devices could help mitigate the ongoing crisis. Future research is planned using additional mobile sensors, geospatial coordination, and machine learning to optimize emergency responses. The lessons from applying the NalPAL extension to the challenging opioid crisis are now being adapted to PALs for other populations and risks including health, travel, recreation, and disasters.
