KW  - Flexible sensor
KW  - Self-healing hydrogels
KW  - Superhydrophobic fabric
KW  - Wearable
KW  - COORDINATION
AB  - Hydrogel-based wearable strain sensors have garnered significant attention due to their flexibility, high sensitivity, and compatibility with human motion monitoring. However, conventional hydrogels suffer from irreversible mechanical damage, limited environmental adaptability, and compromised functionality under harsh conditions such as chemical exposure or prolonged deformation. Herein, we propose a mechanically interlocked hydrogel-textile composite sensor (MIHCS) integrating autonomic self-healing, a synergistic carbon black/silver nanowires hybrid conductive network, and persistent superhydrophobicity. Such sensor comprises a poly(vinyl alcohol)-poly(acrylic acid) hydrogel grafted with polydopamine microcapsules, enabling rapid (>98 %) self-recovery within 12 h at 25 degrees C through dynamic reversible bonding mechanisms upon mechanical damage. Moreover, the topologically entangled superhydrophobic fabric anchored to the hydrogel surface restores superhydrophobicity upon healing, while maintaining contact angle >150.3 degrees and sliding angle <9.4 degrees even after 800 deformation cycles or exposure to extreme pH 1-13. The resulting sensor exhibits excellent sensing linearity (>99.6 %), rapid response (similar to 70 ms), and stable signal output under water jet impact, repeated strain, and corrosive media. By decoupling hydrogel self-healing from fabric reattachment, this work advances durable, maintenance-free wearable devices for healthcare, robotics, and extreme-environment applications.
