AB  - Micro/nanoporous structured flexible pressure sensors are celebrated for their high sensitivity and swift response times, making them indispensable in fields such as biomedical engineering and human-machine interfaces. However, the fabrication of such sensors, while they need to be reproducible, scalable, and cost-effective, continues to present challenges. Herein, we introduce a method that employs poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA) as a sacrificial layer to produce highly sensitive, miniaturized, and flexible microporous sensors in a scalable and straightforward manner. Utilizing screen printing, we deposit a conductive polymer onto the UV (ultraviolet)-laser micromachined, water-soluble PVA layers, which act as a template for the micropore structure. The strategic dissolution of the PVA layer forms a well-defined porous structure, enhancing both the shape control and scalability of the micropores. The resulting sensors, only 480 mu m thick, exhibit a significant sensitivity enhancement (219%) and a 270% improvement in reproducibility compared with conventional pressure sensors. The versatility of our method is further demonstrated through its application in wearable pressure sensors and tactile sensor arrays, effectively detecting subtle physiological signals such as blood pulses and environmental interactions like object weight and human touch.
